and the towns had the authority when they were either in orange or red status in terms of COVID

01:10:31.175 --> 01:10:32.055
cases

01:10:32.295 --> 01:10:34.215
to revert back to

01:10:36.295 --> 01:10:38.375
basically status number two.

01:10:42.135 --> 01:10:50.210
And the state has taken that responsibility back from the individual towns and said all of Connecticut is reverted to phase 2.1.

01:10:50.770 --> 01:10:53.330
That doesn't have any huge impacts

01:10:53.650 --> 01:10:54.930
on the town.

01:10:55.170 --> 01:11:00.210
The things that are the biggest impacts on individuals is that private gatherings,

01:11:00.565 --> 01:11:06.084
like gatherings in your home are supposed to be capped at 10 people now.

01:11:06.805 --> 01:11:11.605
Not a lot of impact on restaurants other than they have to go back down to half capacity

01:11:11.845 --> 01:11:12.565
from

01:11:12.805 --> 01:11:14.085
three quarter capacity.

01:11:15.580 --> 01:11:21.580
Not really a big effect on churches here in town or other businesses or particularly the town hall.

01:11:22.060 --> 01:11:26.860
So you're also looking at the screen at a graph showing

01:11:27.445 --> 01:11:30.165
Andover's COVID positivity rate.

01:11:30.245 --> 01:11:40.165
I took this straight from the raw data and you could see for a period of time, we actually edged into red status, which is more than fifteen cases

01:11:41.960 --> 01:11:44.840
per day per 100,000 population.

01:11:45.240 --> 01:11:48.520
The weird thing is the state does it on this rolling

01:11:48.760 --> 01:11:50.600
fourteen day average

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:57.435
to kind of smooth the data out. And they only update it once a week.

01:11:57.675 --> 01:12:01.195
So by the time we updated it, we were already orange.

01:12:01.435 --> 01:12:03.755
And then they updated it again.

01:12:04.955 --> 01:12:09.755
And we're basically not even orange again. We're back to relatively baseline.

01:12:10.880 --> 01:12:12.480
We had one case

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:18.720
several days ago and we had one case about fourteen days ago. So given that we're

01:12:19.280 --> 01:12:23.680
at a pretty low level and not showing any significant community transmission,

01:12:24.685 --> 01:12:31.005
which is really good. So I'm suggesting that we don't really have any major changes in

01:12:31.165 --> 01:12:34.125
the posture for the town hall at this point.

01:12:34.765 --> 01:12:36.925
I think we need to be prepared

01:12:36.925 --> 01:12:39.725
to react quickly if we start getting spikes

01:12:40.770 --> 01:12:43.170
Because everybody around us is spiking.

01:12:43.490 --> 01:12:50.130
The state as a whole is averaging 14 to 16 cases per day per 100,000 right now.

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:53.250
So on average, about 70%

01:12:53.250 --> 01:12:58.715
of the state is in the red what they're calling like the red category.

01:12:59.755 --> 01:13:03.195
So the other thing that affects us is the

01:13:05.435 --> 01:13:08.315
governor put out executive order nine h

01:13:08.650 --> 01:13:11.610
which has an impact on how we conduct

01:13:11.770 --> 01:13:12.650
meetings.

01:13:13.530 --> 01:13:16.490
Basically it says any in person meeting

01:13:16.730 --> 01:13:18.970
that you have, if anybody

01:13:19.290 --> 01:13:20.650
on that meeting

01:13:20.730 --> 01:13:22.170
wants to attend remotely,

01:13:22.505 --> 01:13:27.065
you have to have the capability in place so anybody can attend remotely,

01:13:27.625 --> 01:13:28.745
that meeting,

01:13:28.905 --> 01:13:30.745
which is going to affect,

01:13:31.225 --> 01:13:35.225
if we do hold a public meeting or a town meeting

01:13:35.400 --> 01:13:37.240
for the land acquisition

01:13:37.480 --> 01:13:38.440
that would

01:13:39.240 --> 01:13:40.760
cause us some issues.

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:48.040
So I've asked Dennis O'Brien to weigh in on that. He and myself and Carol Lee have kind of

01:13:48.395 --> 01:13:53.195
pushed this around for a while trying to figure out how to do this in a reasonable fashion.

01:13:54.395 --> 01:13:54.955
So

01:13:55.435 --> 01:14:03.515
basically what we have to be able to do is conduct a town meeting and still be compliant with the executive orders as well as our charter.

01:14:05.120 --> 01:14:05.840
So,

01:14:06.160 --> 01:14:10.400
and we also consulted with Rob Miller and the health department for that.

01:14:11.120 --> 01:14:11.680
What

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:15.999
we've kind of come up with and Dennis can elaborate

01:14:16.560 --> 01:14:17.440
is

01:14:17.835 --> 01:14:23.035
doing a kind of a split meeting where the first part of the meeting occurs

01:14:23.035 --> 01:14:24.235
via Zoom.

01:14:25.515 --> 01:14:29.835
But we don't check IDs because that was the big stumbling block.

01:14:30.590 --> 01:14:34.110
We ensure that there's plenty of people that go to the meeting,

01:14:34.350 --> 01:14:37.230
we let them all in, you know, from the waiting room,

01:14:37.390 --> 01:14:38.990
we conduct the meeting,

01:14:39.390 --> 01:14:47.625
and after that, so we would still have to elect the moderator, do all that, and then we would conduct a drive by vote

01:14:47.785 --> 01:14:49.225
at the town hall,

01:14:49.465 --> 01:14:52.745
where you basically drive by, everybody's in mask,

01:14:53.145 --> 01:14:55.545
you pull up to the

01:14:56.040 --> 01:15:00.999
first spot, you show your ID, you get checked off the list as a valid voter,

01:15:01.400 --> 01:15:07.959
you go to the next station, you're handed a poll, you fill it out, you stick it in the box, you drive away,

01:15:08.200 --> 01:15:09.879
and then it gets tabulated

01:15:10.040 --> 01:15:11.080
and announced.

01:15:11.755 --> 01:15:14.715
So it's not ideal but it's doable

01:15:14.875 --> 01:15:22.155
under all the guidelines we have and gets us away from the security problems that we potentially have

01:15:22.635 --> 01:15:24.475
doing it the way we did it before.

